Ladybird paints -webkit-text-stroke as a centered glyph outline. The
fill precedes the stroke unless paint-order reverses them. Blink uses
DrawTextPasses() in GraphicsContext, WebKit uses the text painting
methods of TextPainter, and Gecko uses DrawTextRun() in nsTextFrame.cpp
to order the passes. Ladybird uses miter joins like Blink and WebKit.
Gecko instead paints glyph strokes with round joins.

Ladybird preserves fractional widths. The thin, medium, and thick
keywords depend on the font and effective zoom. In Blink, the converter
ConvertTextStrokeWidth() in StyleBuilderConverter computes these values
from 1/48, 3/48, and 5/48 of an em. WebKit’s operator() conversion for
WebkitTextStrokeWidth does the same. Gecko computes the widths in
LineWidth::to_computed_value(), using fixed 1px, 3px, and 5px lengths.
Ladybird follows Chrome and WebKit for these prefixed keyword values.

Ladybird casts a fill-shaped text shadow even when the fill is
transparent. Blink removes shadows from the stroke pass through its
DrawTextPasses(), and Gecko draws the shadow with zero stroke width in
nsTextFrame::PaintOneShadow(). WebKit applies the shadow to the fill
pass in drawSkiaText() in GraphicsContextSkia.

Ladybird suppresses strokes inside highlight rectangles and preserves
the original glyph ink outside them, matching Chrome’s rendering of
partial selections. Gecko suppresses strokes on highlighted segments in
PaintTextWithSelectionColors() in nsTextFrame. WebKit reads the
selection style’s stroke in computeTextSelectionPaintStyle(). Ladybird
follows Chrome for the highlighted outline and its overflow.

With a nonzero text stroke, Ladybird paints currentColor decorations in
a visible explicit stroke color, falling back to the fill color. Blink
makes that choice in DecorationColorIncludingFallback(). WebKit’s
TextDecorationColor specialization of ColorPropertyTraits prefers a
visible stroke, but otherwise uses color. Gecko’s GetTextDecorations()
in nsTextFrame resolves the decoration color directly. Ladybird follows
Chrome for stroked text, keeping explicit decoration colors intact. With
zero stroke width, Ladybird keeps its color-based fallback; that matches
WebKit and Gecko, while Blink uses the text fill color.
